How to translate:
1. Voice first. Each character keeps one consistent voice. Match register, slang and swearing level: do not soften crude words, do not add swearing that is not there. Clipped or dialect spellings in the source mean casual speech: render that with natural {{.LangName}} colloquial forms, not with standard grammar and not with fake phonetic spelling.
2. Verbal tics: if a character repeats an interjection or filler, choose one {{.LangName}} equivalent that sounds natural as a tic and use it identically every time it occurs.
3. Jokes over words. Puns, double meanings and idioms must be adapted: find a {{.LangName}} line that gets the same laugh at the same moment, even if the words change. A literal translation of a joke that no longer works is a failure. Keep every piece of plot information other panels rely on.
4. Signs, brand names, plates and names: keep them exactly as drawn unless the reference sheet says the sign carries meaning that a {{.LangName}} reader would miss. Scrambled-brand gags work in any language: keep them as drawn. Translate a sign only if the joke needs it. Invented or parody names that read as words (made-up band, brand or character names built from real words) are part of the joke: localize them into {{.LangName}} names that land the same way.
5. Sound effects: use the sound a {{.LangName}} comic would letter; keep it if it already works.
6. Fit: the text must fit the bubble. At most maxChars characters and settable in at most maxLines lines of about the original line length. Shorter is better than cramped; cut filler, never cut the joke. Do not insert line breaks, the letterer fits the text.
7. Case: if the source is capitals, write the translation in capitals (keep accents and umlauts).
8. Punctuation and rhythm: comics use "...", "!", "?!" and dashes for timing. Keep the timing of the original beats.

For every region return:
- "literal": a faithful, plain {{.LangName}} rendering of what the source literally says (for the editor; ignore the budget)
- "text": the final adapted lettering
- "notes": one short sentence if "text" departs from "literal" or a pun/tic/sign decision was made; otherwise ""

